WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:15.991
September 4, 1876, London Dear Diary, When Jonathan told me that he had been asked to travel to Transylvania on

2
00:00:15.992 --> 00:00:25.031
business, I was devastated because I knew that it meant we might have to postpone our autumn wedding plans. Regardless,

3
00:00:25.032 --> 00:00:28.952
I encouraged him to go, hoping that he could use this opportunity to secure more business from Count Dracula in

4
00:00:28.953 --> 00:00:37.309
the future. Still, I was worried that I would be lonely without him. To pass the time, I went

5
00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:45.412
to visit my best friend, Lucy, at her seaside home in Whitby, a summer resort town north of London. Her

6
00:00:45.413 --> 00:00:52.404
boyfriend, Arthur, had just asked her to marry him, so we had a lot to discuss and plan for her

7
00:00:52.405 --> 00:01:02.285
nuptials, as well as my own. "Jonathan wrote me a letter from Transylvania, but it’s very strangely worded," I complained

8
00:01:02.286 --> 00:01:06.460
to Lucy one day. "Maybe he’s ill," she replied, but in the next breath, she continued gushing over her beloved

9
00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:16.079
Arthur, describing how he’d proposed to her on the shore at dusk. She always talked about him and I felt

10
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:24.563
myself becoming jealous. Why had Jonathan left me for so long? And why were his letters so short and strange?

11
00:01:24.564 --> 00:01:32.903
I was angry with him, yet worried for him as well. The sea air smelled so fresh and wonderful

12
00:01:32.904 --> 00:01:42.038
that it revived my senses after so many long, dreary, English winter days spent in London. Lucy and I took

13
00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:49.673
walks together each afternoon before teatime, along the beach where the calm waves from the North Sea washed up on

14
00:01:49.674 --> 00:01:52.514
shore. The sky seemed perpetually blue, with no rain for many weeks, until one night, a terrible thunderstorm came in

15
00:01:52.515 --> 00:01:55.014
off the coast. The next morning, a giddy Lucy banged on my patio doors, waking me up early, and told

16
00:01:55.015 --> 00:02:00.943
me to put on my robe. "Come out here on the shore!" she called. "Look! Look at the ship!" When

17
00:02:00.944 --> 00:02:07.784
I arrived at her side on the beach a few minutes later, I could barely believe my own eyes. There

18
00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:13.967
on the shore, just a few feet from her property, was a large ship that must have crashed in the

19
00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:19.378
night. The townspeople were gathered around it and Lucy asked a young man if there was anyone on board.

20
00:02:19.386 --> 00:02:28.774
"Nobody," said the young man. "There wasn’t anyone on board, dead or alive. But there were about fifty wooden coffins

21
00:02:28.775 --> 00:02:31.155
filled with soil on the lower deck and when we went to open the door, a large black dog jumped

22
00:02:31.156 --> 00:02:35.676
out. He ran off into the woods." Everyone in town was talking about the mysterious ship that afternoon. "It’s a

23
00:02:35.677 --> 00:02:39.564
Russian ship," I overheard one man tell his friend. "Yup, it looks like it’s come across the Black Sea from

24
00:02:39.565 --> 00:02:43.786
Varna. There are logs, but nobody has filled them out for weeks. A realtor from London came to collect the

25
00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:47.691
cargo this afternoon, but even they don’t know what happened to the captain and crew─it’s as if they had vanished

26
00:02:47.692 --> 00:02:57.508
into thin air!" "Has anyone seen the black dog?" I asked. They just shook their heads. Sometime after midnight

27
00:02:57.509 --> 00:03:00.476
that night, I awoke to find my bedroom patio doors open and a cool breeze blowing into the room. I

28
00:03:00.477 --> 00:03:03.227
got up to close them, but when I looked out, I saw Lucy skipping across the lawn toward the woods.

29
00:03:03.228 --> 00:03:11.386
Lucy had once told me a story of how she loved to play in the woods as a child, close

30
00:03:11.387 --> 00:03:17.616
to the cemetery out by the old abandoned parish down the road. I was overcome with worry─maybe she was walking

31
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:26.751
in her sleep and didn’t know what she was doing? I promptly pulled on my robe and hurried out into

32
00:03:26.752 --> 00:03:33.275
the yard to follow her. When I finally caught up to Lucy, she was indeed at the parish in the

33
00:03:33.276 --> 00:03:38.068
cemetery, but her mannerisms were childlike and strange. She was sitting on top of a gravestone, humming a song

34
00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:46.243
to herself; her eyes were closed and she was swinging her feet like a child. Behind her, a figure moved

35
00:03:46.244 --> 00:03:54.537
in the shadows─the dog! Then, all of a sudden, the black dog leaped out of the woods and attacked Lucy’s

36
00:03:54.538 --> 00:03:59.219
neck! She let out a shrieking scream that scared it off. Then she tumbled from the gravestone, landing hard on

37
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:07.687
the ground, and lost consciousness. I called for Arthur’s help immediately, and he came rushing to Lucy’s side, knelt down,

38
00:04:07.696 --> 00:04:13.203
and picked her up in his strong arms. Together, we carried her into her room and put her in bed.

39
00:04:13.204 --> 00:04:21.092
She had lost all the color in her face, but had only two small puncture wounds on her neck, which

40
00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:25.171
were dripping with blood. Lucy’s mother telephoned their family doctor, Jack Seward, and we waited anxiously for his arrival

41
00:04:25.172 --> 00:04:32.592
the next morning. Unfortunately, by the time his coach pulled up, Lucy’s condition had worsened─she was awake, but appeared to

42
00:04:32.593 --> 00:04:39.973
be in a trance, just staring out the window toward the woods and humming a little tune to herself. The

43
00:04:39.974 --> 00:04:53.592
strange illness persisted. The doctor and I took turns staying with Lucy through each night, watching for any changes. One

44
00:04:53.593 --> 00:05:01.640
evening, I fell asleep in the chair next to her bed, and in the middle of the night, I was

45
00:05:01.641 --> 00:05:09.354
awoken by a noise. Lucy was standing next to the window and talking out loud to herself. "Come to me,"

46
00:05:09.355 --> 00:05:16.394
she said. "I will give you what you want . . ." I stood up and walked over to her,

47
00:05:16.395 --> 00:05:24.894
but before I could stop her, she opened the window and tried to jump out. I pulled her back

48
00:05:24.895 --> 00:05:35.640
to the bed, and as I did, out of the corner of my eye, I saw the large black dog

49
00:05:35.641 --> 00:05:45.743
pacing back and forth in the woods. I shuddered with fear. Finally, after many stressful days, I received a letter

50
00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:52.029
from Jonathan telling me that he was in Bistritz and would be taking the next boat home to England. However,

51
00:05:52.030 --> 00:06:03.013
my excitement was dashed because, after a fortnight, he had still not arrived in London. Then, one chilly morning, I

52
00:06:03.014 --> 00:06:07.735
received notice from a hospital in Budapest that my fiancé was in their care after arriving on foot with nothing

53
00:06:07.736 --> 00:06:15.275
but the clothes on his back. Poor Jonathan was quite ill─just as Lucy had suspected! I told Arthur about the

54
00:06:15.276 --> 00:06:22.084
situation, and he insisted that he would nurse Lucy so that I could go take care of Jonathan. At the

55
00:06:22.085 --> 00:06:26.377
Budapest hospital, a sympathetic nurse led me down a long corridor and into Jonathan’s room. "He’s quite dehydrated and

56
00:06:26.378 --> 00:06:32.750
his clothes are ragged," she explained. "We think that he walked here on foot from Transylvania, but he won’t acknowledge

57
00:06:32.751 --> 00:06:36.307
any of our questions. He only asked that we send notice to you that he was here." Oh, my poor

58
00:06:36.308 --> 00:06:46.275
Jonathan! He was so frail and thin compared to when he had left for the castle in the spring, and

59
00:06:46.276 --> 00:06:49.648
I just collapsed in his arms crying. "Why won’t you tell us what happened?" I begged. But, instead of responding,

60
00:06:49.649 --> 00:06:56.926
he just handed me his diary and motioned for me to sit in the chair next to his bed. In

61
00:06:56.927 --> 00:07:04.553
the pages of his diary, I was shocked to learn of his adventure in Transylvania and the horrible descriptions of

62
00:07:04.554 --> 00:07:13.696
the count. The next morning, I held him tight in my arms and said, "Oh, Jonathan, I’m so happy

63
00:07:13.697 --> 00:07:15.688
that you are safe and far away from that man." "Yes, Mina," he replied. "As am I . . .

64
00:07:15.689 --> 00:07:19.450
I never wish to see him again." As soon as Jonathan was well enough to walk again, we returned to

65
00:07:19.451 --> 00:07:28.506
England by ship, and were married shortly after in one of the largest churches in London. Our family and friends

66
00:07:28.507 --> 00:07:38.275
were delighted to see Jonathan healthy again after his great ordeal, and asked him many questions about his experience in

67
00:07:38.276 --> 00:07:45.854
Transylvania. But no matter how many people approached him wanting a story, he never spoke of his time at Castle

68
00:07:45.855 --> 00:07:56.902
Dracula again. Our lives seemed to finally be settling into place when, one sunny afternoon, there was a knock at

69
00:07:56.903 --> 00:07:58.799
the door, and I opened it to discover a letter had been left on the doorstep. It was from

70
00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:07.974
Arthur. I ripped it open quickly, hoping that it would be news about his wedding to Lucy, but wasn’t an

71
00:08:07.975 --> 00:08:16.362
invitation─it was something I hadn’t expected! The good doctor Seward had tried everything to treat my dear friend Lucy, but

72
00:08:16.363 --> 00:08:21.476
his efforts had failed─Lucy was dead. I told Jonathan, and we made plans to attend Lucy’s funeral the next week.

